{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "f1a9c3e2-7d4b-4a8e-9c1f-tcos-fullsuite-01",
    "name": "TCOS Finance Tracking — Full Suite (Runner)",
    "description": "Consolidates the 4 domain collections (Auth, Admin Staff, Project & Source, Payments & Reimbursement Approval) into one collection that runs start-to-finish in a single Postman Collection Runner pass, in dependency order.\n\n**Excluded on purpose**: every Supabase-Auth-dependent request (Claim, Login via Supabase, the \"Get Supabase session\" dev helper) — those need a manually-provisioned external Supabase Auth user and can't run unattended. They still live in `TCOS-Auth.postman_collection.json` if you need them.\n\n**Two more real, unavoidable gaps in unattended runs** (both pre-existing limitations of the individual collections too, not new here):\n1. **Reset Password** needs a `reset_token` copied out of the API server's console log (no email provider wired up, no token returned via the API by design) — it will 400 on a cold run unless you paste one in first. Non-fatal to the rest of the run either way.\n2. **CSV imports** (Staff Import, Reimbursement Import) need a real file attached to the request's Body tab — Postman collection JSON can't embed a file. They'll 400 \"file required\" on an unattended run. Attach `api/tests/fixtures/staff-import.csv` / a reimbursement CSV manually before running those two requests specifically if you want them to pass.\n\nEverything else runs clean end-to-end with zero manual setup beyond a reachable Postgres (the hosted Supabase project is already live) with the seed data applied. Folder order matters — it mirrors real domain dependencies (Payments & Reimbursements needs real staff/department/project rows that the earlier folders don't create, so it deliberately reuses seeded IDs instead of `created_project_id` from folder 3).\n\nDynamic values (`{{$timestamp}}`) are used for anything that would otherwise 409 on a second run (staff email, project name, reimbursement tracking_id) so this collection is safely re-runnable.\n\n**Real constraint found by actually running this against the live server**: `RateLimit.middleware.js`'s `authAttempts` limiter caps `/auth/login` + `/auth/verify-password` COMBINED at 5 requests per 15 minutes per IP (doc 03 §4, real security behavior, not a bug). This collection alone makes ~9 such calls across its 4 folders (3 plain logins in folders 1-3, plus chompoo/golf/beam logins and 2 step-up verifies in folder 4). A single Runner pass WILL 429 partway through once the 5th combined login/verify-password call fires in that window, cascading to 401s on everything downstream that needed a token it never got — that's the limiter doing its job, not a collection defect. Either wait out the 15-minute window between full passes, restart the API server (resets the in-memory store), or — if unattended full-suite runs need to be routine — ask chompoo/the team whether `authAttempts` should be loosened specifically for non-production `NODE_ENV` before changing anything security-relevant.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
